  "authoringNote": "The predicate is the declared accessStatus unreadable (review answer 2), not an unsupported MIME type or an invalid body: the metadata is preserved with MIME application/json, and the held bytes are a well-formed encounter body whose SHA-256 equals contentHash. Under R09's first-condition order the version is not restricted and its hash matches, so its one condition is R09.access_unreadable, unreadable for the authorization coordinator, and it is the R09 parent (C07). The held body is never parsed into facts: no date, practitioner, clinical statement or LCMP flag from it is cited. No final current usable encounter exists and the unreadable version is a candidate (C09), so R04 holds R04.encounter_unreadable, unreadable for the authorization coordinator, naming it as the R04 parent, with no false absence. Invalid bodies (R09 body_invalid, F01x3) and unsupported MIME (F08x4) are covered elsewhere. R04 reads only final current usable documents (recorded reading of C05 and C09), so it cites the final order's status, date and practitioner identifier (three) and nothing from the encounter. R02 binds the encounter by its metadata subject only and cites the order and receipt beneficiary facts (four). R01 (eight citations), R03 (nine) and R05 (four; the receipt follows the order date span 2026-09-01T10:00Z to 2026-09-03T12:00Z under C06) pass. R06 stays qualified clinical review with no clinical citation and no administrative effect: an unavailable record is not evidence that any clinical fact is absent, and no negative clinical assertion is made. R07 passes with no citation because neither the case nor a current usable encounter states LCMP incorporation; that pass asserts nothing about the content of the unavailable record. R08 passes and R10 is not applicable. The state is needs_information, blocked by R04 and R09.",
